In a frame rotating with angular velocity $bold(omega)$, a particle at position $bold(x)$ experiences the centrifugal force
$
  bold(F_"cent") = -m bold(omega) times (bold(omega) times bold(x)),
$
which points away from the axis of rotation. Its size is
$
  abs(bold(F_"cent")) = m omega^2 r cos theta,
$
where $r$ is the distance from the centre of the Earth and $theta$ is the angle between $bold(x)$ and the equatorial plane.

The centrifugal force is conservative:
$
  bold(F_"cent") = - nabla V_"cent", quad V_"cent" = -(m)/(2) abs(bold(omega) times bold(x))^2 = -(m)/(2) omega^2 r^2 cos^2 theta,
$
so potential energy is lowered by moving away from the axis of rotation.
